cation if no improvement.\n   - **Additional Testing:** Order fasting blood sugar and hemoglobin A1c.\n\n2. **Hypertension:**\n   - **Medical Reasoning:** Blood pressures have been normal.\n   - **Patient Education and Counseling:** Continue current medication regimen (Li- Lisinopril 10 mg once daily).\n   - **Medical Treatment:** No changes to current antihypertensive medication.\n\n**Patient Agreements:** The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n**Instructions:**\n- The patient is advised to check her blood sugars in the morning and call them in for the first fasting blood sugar in about 2 weeks.\n- She will return for a follow-up in 2 months for a re-evaluation of her blood sugar levels and overall management.\n##### }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Beverly is a 24 year old female with a history of type 2 diabetes mellitus and hypertension. She presented for her yearly exam. She had not been seen in a year. She began working in a bakery that summer and found herself snacking a lot during shifts and eating a lot more desserts than she normally would. Her blood sugars were up and down because of diet, she attributed, because she was taking her medication as prescribed. She had not missed anything else. The only thing that was different was her diet, pretty much. Because of the new job and all that. She found it really challenging to resist the temptation. She had no willpower like me? (laughs)\nShe had high blood pressure, hypertension. How were her blood sugars? Had she been checking them? She said her blood pressure\'s been good. Um, despite her, um, her diabetes levels being up and down. So, that\'s been good. She had no concerns about hurting herself or anything like that. She was getting a full eight hours of sleep. No swelling in her neck. No swelling in her legs. No swelling in her belly. She had no pain. She had no fevers. She had no cough. She had no shortness of breath. She had no chest pain. She had no abdomina