 distress.\n- **Vital Signs:** No fever noted.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Swelling of the elbow joints bilaterally with pain to palpation.\n- **Skin:** Bull\'s-eye rash on the left abdomen, warm to palpation, non-tender to palpation.\n- **Cardiovascular:** Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs.\n- **Respiratory:** Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n\n**Laboratory Results:**\n- **Lyme Titer:** Elevated\n- **CBC:** Normal white blood cell count\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n- **Primary Diagnosis:** Lyme Disease, confirmed by elevated Lyme titer.\n- **Secondary Diagnoses:**\n  - Type 2 Diabetes, well-managed on metformin.\n  - Hypertension, well-managed on lisinopril.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n- **Lyme Disease:**\n  - **Medical Reasoning:** Elevated Lyme titer confirms diagnosis of Lyme disease.\n  - **Medical Treatment:** Prescribe doxycycline 100mg BID for 3 weeks.\n  - **Patient Education and Counseling:** Educate the patient on the importance of completing the full course of antibiotics and monitoring for any worsening symptoms.\n  - **Referral:** Referral to an infectious disease specialist, Dr. Emily Carter, for further management of Lyme disease.\n  - **Follow-up:** Follow-up appointment in 1 week.\n\n- **Type 2 Diabetes:**\n  - **Medical Reasoning:** Blood sugars appear stable but monitoring is recommended.\n  - **Medical Treatment:** Order hemoglobin A1c.\n  - **Patient Education and Counseling:** Reinforce the importance of blood sugar monitoring and adherence to diet and medication regimen.\n\n- **Hypertension:**\n  - **Medical Reasoning:** Blood pressure is currently stable.\n  - **Medical Treatment:** Continue lisinopril 10mg daily.\n  - **Patient Education and Counseling:** Encourage continued monitoring of blood pressure at home and adherence to dietary recommendations.\n\n**Patient Agreements:**\nThe patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n**INSTRUCTIONS:**\nThe patient will follow up in 1 week for re-evaluation of symptoms and lab results. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ We are going to go right to the front-. so when you touch that- hi, michelle, how are you?\n[patient] i\'m good. how are you?\n[doctor] i\'m good. so i know the nurse told you about dax. i\'d like to dax a little bit about you, okay?\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] all right. so, michelle is a 31-year-old female with a past medical history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension who presents with abnormal lab finding. so, michelle, it came back to me that you had had a positive long... lyme titer.\n[patient] yeah.\n[doctor] and so, uh, can you, you, you know, can you tell me a little bit about it? i know the last time i saw you, you had had an insect bite, and you know, it came back positive for lyme. so how are you feeling?\n[patient] i\'ve been feeling... i could feel better. um... i\'ve been feeling tired, um, really lacking a lot of energy, and i have swollen joints in my elbows, just really hurts.\n[doctor] okay. all right. and have you had any, any body aches?\n[patient] yeah, i have.\n[doctor] okay. all right. um, and i know that you were out hiking in the woods and, and that type of thing a couple of weeks ago, and we thought that that\'s where you got the bite. so, um, how\'s your, how\'s your appetite? have you been okay? a little nauseous?\n[patient] yeah, i\'ve been a little nauseous. i haven\'t been able to eat much. i\'ve been trying just to keep, you know, toast and crackers and that\'s all about i\'ve been able to stomach, really.\n[doctor] okay, and, and i know that you had had that, that, uh, bite there. have you noticed any other rash anywhere else?\n[patient] yeah, i have.\n[doctor] okay. all right. sometimes they kind of describe it as looking as like a bull\'s-eye.\n[patient] yes.\n[doctor] is that what it looked like?\n[patient] mm-hmm.\n[doctor] okay. all right. well, um, have you had any, any high fevers?\n[patient] uh, no, not that i\'ve noticed.\n[doctor] all right. um, and how about from a diabetes sta