n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Temperature: Not taken during this visit.\n- Heart Rate: Normal.\n- Respiratory Rate: Normal.\n- Blood Pressure: Not taken during this visit.\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- General: Patient appears healthy and in no distress.\n- Respiratory: Normal breath sounds, no wheezing or crackles.\n- ENT: No nasal polyps, normal tympanic membranes.\n- Cardiovascular: Normal heart sounds, no murmurs.\n- Lungs: Normal breath sounds, spirometry results pending.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n- Mild Persistent Asthma, well-controlled on current regimen.\n- Allergic Rhinitis, seasonal.\n- Peanut Allergy, known.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n- Continue Wixela (250/50 mcg, 1 puff twice daily) as prescribed.\n- Monitor and document asthma control, aim to step down to inhaler as needed if possible.\n- Repeat Spirometry in 4 months to assess asthma control.\n- Refill Albuterol (90 mcg, 1 inhaler) as needed for exercise.\n- Refill EpiPen (0.3 mg, 2 auto-injectors) as needed for allergic reactions.\n- Continue Zyrtec (Cetirizine) 10 mg daily during allergy season.\n- Lifestyle change: Encourage reduced tobacco and vaping use, increased physical activity.\n- Follow-up in 4 months with ACT and spirometry.\n\n**Instructions:**\n- The patient will follow up in 4 months with spirometry to assess asthma control. She was advised to continue using Wixela and to monitor her symptoms. If she finds it necessary, she can step down to an inhaler as needed. All prescriptions were called in to pharmacy. The patient was educated on the importance of minimizing tobacco and vaping and was encouraged to increase physical activity. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Next patient is Emily Hernandez. Date of birth, march 26 2001. This is a 20 year old female being seen today for our yearly follow-up for asthma, allergic rhinitis and food allergy. Due to insurance changes since our last office appointment in April 2020, she was switched from symbicort to wixela. She also uses zyrtec in 