rican male with a history of hypertension and palpitations. He reports that his blood pressure was elevated during a recent emergency room visit. He notes that he was previously on amlodipine 10 mg daily but was switched to benicar hct (40-25 mg) one month ago by his new primary care physician. He denies any chest pain or shortness of breath. He believes his symptoms were exacerbated by high caffeine intake on the day of the ER visit and not having breakfast. He has not experienced any recent episodes since the visit.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- Cardiovascular: Reports palpitations during the ER visit, no chest pain.\n- Respiratory: No shortness of breath.\n- Gastrointestinal: No nausea, vomiting, or abdominal pain.\n- Musculoskeletal: Reports walking on the treadmill occasionally.\n- Neurological: No dizziness, headaches, or syncope.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: 146/95 mmHg (home monitoring).\n- Heart Rate: 78 bpm.\n- Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute.\n- Temperature: 98.6°F.\n\n**General Physical Exam:**\n- General: Alert, oriented, no acute distress.\n- Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\n- Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n- Extremities: No edema.\n\n**Investigations:**\n- Blood Work: \n  - Metabolic Panel (awaiting results).\n  - Thyroid Profile (awaiting results from March 1st, 2021).\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n- **Hypertension, Unspecified (I10)**\n  - Patient presents with resistant hypertension despite being on three medications (amlodipine 10 mg daily and benicar hct 40-25 mg daily).\n  - Blood pressure remains elevated at 146/95 mmHg with a home monitoring reading.\n  - Previous thyroid profile ordered (awaiting results from March 1st, 2021).\n  - Elevated potassium at 4.0 mmol/L (awaiting repeat test).\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n- **Medication Adjustment:**\n  - Start spironolactone 25 mg, oral, once daily. Dispense 30 tablets.\n  \n- **Follow-up Recommendations:**\n  - 