enting for a follow-up visit regarding her perioral dermatitis. She has been experiencing mild to moderate symptoms for the past 9 months, with persistent erythema and occasional pustules around the mouth. She reports significant improvement with the use of sulfacetamide wash and metronidazole cream, but has recently been advised to use doxycycline 100 mg orally, once daily. She is currently not breastfeeding and has stopped breastfeeding when she started the doxycycline. She is concerned about the potential side effects of long-term antibiotic use.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- Dermatologic: Mild to moderate erythema and occasional pustules around the mouth.\n- General: No other systemic symptoms reported.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Physical Exam:**\n- Fitzpatrick Skin Type: IV-V\n- Bilateral medial cheeks: Presence of a few faint erythematous papules and mild erythema around and underneath the nostrils.\n\n**Investigations:**\n- No new lab tests or imaging required at this time.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n- Perioral Dermatitis\n- Rosacea\n- Ocular Rosacea\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n**Medications:**\n- Doxycycline 100 mg, oral, once daily.\n- Tacrolimus 0.1% ointment, apply to affected area twice daily until resolved, then once daily for one week, then discontinue.\n\n**Follow-up Recommendations:**\n- Follow-up in 4 weeks.\n\n**Lifestyle Change:**\n- Advise gentle skin care regimen, avoiding harsh soaps and exfoliating products.\n\n**Instructions:**\n- Use sulfacetamide wash twice daily.\n- Apply metronidazole cream immediately after washing the face.\n- Continue doxycycline 100 mg, oral, once daily.\n- Use tacrolimus 0.1% ointment to affected area twice daily until resolved, then once daily for one week, then discontinue.\n\n**Referral:**\n- Referral to Dr. Emily Johnson, Dermatologist, for further evaluation and management of perioral dermatitis and rosacea.\n\n**Patient Education:**\n- Discussed potential side effects of doxycycline, including gastrointestinal upset and sun sensitivity.\n- Emphasized the importance of adhering to the prescribed treatment regimen.\n- Advised on gentle skin care practices to prevent exacerbation of symptoms.\n\n**Treatment Disparities:**\n- Discussed cost-effective alternatives due to limited insurance coverage, including the option to switch to a generic form of tacrolimus ointment if necessary.\n\n**Instructions for the Patient:**\n- Continue with the current treatment plan.\n- Use gentle skin care products.\n- Follow up in 4 weeks.\n- If symptoms worsen or new symptoms develop, reach out to the healthcare provider.\n\n**Signature:**\nDr. [Your Name], MD  \nDermatologist  \n[Date] }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Patient’s name is Jacqueline Miller, mrn49282721. Last visit exam is used where appropriate.\nDoctor: Hi, Jacqueline, how are you doing today?\nPatient: I’m pretty. Good. How are you?\nDoctor: I’m good, thank you. So it sounds like we’re, um, under good control right now.\nPatient: Yes, it’s doing much better.\nDoctor: Good, good. Do you have any rash leftover?\nPatient: Yeah, I have a- a small bit leftover. I started using, uh, doxycycline only one a day because I think the pharmacist said if it’s getting better, to just limit it to once a day.\nDoctor: Okay, that sounds good.\nPatient: But I was taking it twice a day and I did notice a lotta improvement.\nDoctor: Okay.\nPatient: And then when I started doing it only once a day, it seems the same and it’s not continuing to get better. So it’s kinda plateaued a little bit.\nDoctor: I see. um, are you breastfeeding at this time?\nPatient: No, I’m not right now.\nDoctor: Okay, good. So you- you should not be breastfeeding while you’re on that medication.\nPatient: Yeah, actually I stopped breastfeeding and then asked for the doxycycline at that time.\nDoctor: Okay. even with the doxycycline, you- you should keep using the elidel.\nPatient: Okay. and I did n’t take the elidel because when I read about it I got worried. So I wanted to jus