t upper quadrant pain for several weeks, which has been associated with nausea and low-grade fever. The pain occurs daily, often after meals, and significantly impacts his ability to perform daily activities. The patient is a type 2 diabetic and was previously advised to follow a diet and exercise regimen to manage his condition. He has recently transitioned to a ketogenic diet and has noticed an improvement in his symptoms, including weight loss of 25 lbs over the last 3 months.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- **Constitutional:** Reports weight loss of 25 lbs over the last 3 months.\n- **Gastrointestinal:** Reports moderate to severe right upper quadrant pain, nausea, and low-grade fever. No vomiting.\n- **Endocrine:** History of type 2 diabetes mellitus.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Reports minimal exercise due to pain.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: 128/88 mmHg\n- Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths/min\n- Pulse Rate: 68 bpm\n- Temperature: Afebrile\n- Oxygen Saturation: 99% on room air\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- **Cardiovascular:** Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, gallops, or rubs.\n- **Respiratory:** Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n- **Abdominal:** Soft, nondistended, positive bowel sounds. Mild guarding noted in the right upper quadrant. Positive for Murphy\'s sign. No rebound tenderness or peritoneal signs.\n- **Neurological:** Alert and oriented, no focal deficits.\n\n**Investigation/Test Results:**\n- **Abdominal Ultrasound:** Multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder, mild thickening of the gallbladder wall. Common bile duct size within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n- **Diagnosis:** Calculus of gallbladder with acute and chronic cholecystitis without obstruction.\n- **Medical Reasoning:** The patient presents with classic symptoms of cholecystitis, including right upper quadrant pain, nausea, and low-grade fever. The ultrasound findings confirm the presence of gallstones and mild gallbladder wall thickening, consistent with chronic cholecystitis.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n- **Surgical Treatment:** Laparoscopic cholecystectomy is planned and scheduled.\n- **Patient Education and Counseling:** Discussed the procedure, expected outcomes, and postoperative care. Advised the patient to avoid high-fat foods post-surgery and to follow a diet high in fiber.\n- **Follow-Up:** Postoperative follow-up is scheduled for 1 week to assess recovery and wound healing.\n- **Additional Recommendations:** Continue monitoring and management of type 2 diabetes mellitus. Follow-up with the endocrinologist for diabetes management as outlined in the current treatment plan.\n\nThe patient understands and agrees with the proposed treatment plan and follow-up schedule. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Bobby is a sixty six year old male who presented to Dr. Miller who is his primary care physician for a chief complaint of right upper quadrant pain that has been ongoing for several weeks. He underwent an abdominal ultrasound which showed multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder and was recommended to avoid several high fat foods. He was referred to see me for a second opinion. He was in significant pain at that time and was sent for a CT scan of the abdomen. CT scan showed a distended gallbladder with multiple gallstones present. He was diagnosed with gallstones and was recommended to have a cholecystectomy. He was sent home on a low fat diet and was scheduled for surgery. He had a laparoscopic cholecystectomy and did well postoperatively. He had a follow up with me one week later and was doing well. He was asymptomatic at that time.\nBobby is a tall and thin gentleman who is sixty six years old. He is a type two diabetic who was originally going to handle that with diet and exercise. He did not make lifestyle changes and his diabetes got worse and he was started on medication for his diabetes. He is a retired police officer who works part time at a local restaurant. He is very active outdoors and enjoys photography.\nBobby presented to me with a chief complaint of right upper quadrant pain. He described the pain as severe and it was located underneath his right rib cage. It was a squeezing type of pain that came and went. When it came it was very severe and he would get nauseated and sick to his stomach. He would rate that pain an eight on a scale of zero to ten with ten being the worst pain he has ever been in his life. It would wax and wane and was usually around mealtimes. It would also come on a couple of hours after he ate. It was related to eating.\nHe was a little bit overweight but was in no distress. His vital signs were one twenty eight over eighty eight, respiratory rate of sixteen, pulse rate of sixty eight, and he was afebrile. His oxygen saturation on room air was ninety nine percent. He had regular rate and rhythm of his heart. No ectopic beats, no clicks, rubs, or murmurs were noted. His lungs were clear and equal bilaterally to auscultation. He had soft nondistended abdomen and positive bowel sounds. He was positive for guarding there to the right upper quadrant but did not have rebound tenderness. When I pressed slightly beneath his ribs on the right hand side he would guard and he did hurt. He was positive for murphy’s signs but did not have any peritoneal signs.\nHis abdominal ultrasound showed multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. There was mild thickening of the gallbladder. The common bile duct size was within normal limits at four. one millimeters. The liver and spleen were normal. There were no }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nAbdominal pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of type 2 diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports enjoying nature photography.\n\nFAMILY HISTORY\n\nPatient reports family history of cholelithiasis. Both of his parents have had cholecystectomies.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Reports low-grade fevers.\nGastrointestinal: Reports right-sided abdominal pain and nausea. Denies vomiting.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 128/88 mmHg.\nRespiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute.\nHeart Rate: 68 beats per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 99% on room air.\nBody Temperature: Afebrile.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear and equal bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No ectopic beats. No murmurs, clicks, or rubs.\n\nGastrointestinal\n- Examination of Abdomen: Soft, nondistended abdomen. Positive slight guarding to the right upper quadrant, but without rebound tenderness. Positive for Murphy signs. Peritoneal signs not appreciated.\n- Auscultation: Bowel sounds normal in all 4 quadrants.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAbdominal ultrasound obtained at an outside facility is reviewed today. This demonstrates multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. I do appreciate some mild thickening. The common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Mild cholecystitis with gallstones.\n- Medical Reasoning: Based on the patient\'s symptoms, exam findings, and ultrasound, I do believe he has some mild cholecystitis with gallstones. I do not appreciate any frank obstruction, but it looks like his gallbladder is inflamed. This appears to have happened maybe over the last few weeks or since he has been symptomatic.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I had a thorough discussion with the patient concerning surgical treatment. The preoperative, intraoperative, and postoperative courses of care were described and discussed. I explained the surgery will take approximately 1 to 1.5 hours, and he will probably spend the night in the hospital for post-procedure monitoring. We also discussed that he should avoid high fat foods after surgery and eat foods that are high in fiber. All questions were answered.\n- Medical Treatment: Since he has not had any improvement with dietary modifications, I recommend a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. A mutually agreed upon surgical date has been scheduled for 1 week.\n\n2. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently managing his diabetes with diet and exercise.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient he should refrain from hiking in the immediate postoperative period; however, he may return to walking outdoors on solid ground 1 week status post-surgery.\nMedical Treatment: I recommended he follow up with his physician regarding his impending surgery. We will continue to monitor his diabetes as has been outlined by either his endocrinologist or his primary care provider.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me 1 week status post-surgery.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
