cchymosis or bruising.\n- **Shoulder:** Pain to palpation of the posterior shoulder at the acromioclavicular joint. Decreased abduction and adduction to 90 degrees. Pain with supination and pronation of the right arm. No radial artery pulse palpable.\n- **Neurological:** Cranial nerves II-XII intact, no focal deficits.\n\n**Investigations:**\n- **Shoulder X-ray:** Right shoulder x-ray reveals an acute acromioclavicular joint fracture.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n**Diagnosis:**\n- Right shoulder pain with acute acromioclavicular joint fracture.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n**Treatment:**\n- **Sling:** Patient to be placed in a sling to immobilize the shoulder.\n- **Medication:** Prescribed meloxicam 15 mg, oral, once a day for pain, quantity 30 tablets.\n- **Imaging:** Order a CT scan of the right shoulder to evaluate the joint more thoroughly.\n- **Physical Therapy:** Referral to physical therapy for shoulder strengthening exercises to avoid surgical intervention.\n\n**Follow-up Recommendations:**\n- Follow-up in 2 weeks for re-evaluation of the shoulder fracture and pain management. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ A 67-year-old Caucasian male presented to the office for a shoulder injury. The patient stated that he was active in his younger years playing football with friends. He decided to pick up tackle football again, but when he got back into the game, he got tackled by bigger kids and he landed on his shoulder one time. He stated that he got back into the game, and he was okay but the past couple of days, it\'s been bothering him. The patient stated that it was probably about four days ago that it happened. He\'s happy that he\'s out there playing tackle football at age 67.\nThe patient stated that it was his right shoulder, and he\'s right handed. He denied any numbing or tingling in his fingers. He stated that his fingers have been okay. He stated that he can grab, but he\'s just trying to avoid lifting anything, yeah anything anything anything a little bit too heavy like he had a chipment command so he had a box in the driveway the other day and reaching down and picking up the box was a little tough.\nThe patient stated that he has pain in the shoulder, and it\'s kinda on the on the back side right right around here. He stated that he can\'t lift up his shoulder so that his shoulder touches his ear. He stated that it hurts a little bit. He stated that he took a little bit of tylenol trying to take it easy. It it kinda like kept it at the same spot that has been but it really didn\'t make it go down. The patient stated that his symptoms are about the same since it happened, and he doesn\'t have any neck problems. The patient stated that the rest of it\'s doing okay it\'s just the just the shoulder. The patient stated that he\'s never had any other orthopedic injuries in the past, and he\'s never injured that shoulder as far as he knows. The patient stated that he had a appendicitis so he had an appendectomy maybe like three four years ago but that\'s about it.\nOn physical exam, the patient was afebrile with a temperature of 98.1 Fahrenheit. He had a blood pressure of one twenty two over seventy six. He had a heart rate of eighty two beats per minute. His oxygenation was ninety five percent on room air. On his cervical spine exam, there was no tenderness to palpation of the cervical spine. There was full range of motion on head exam. There did not appear to be any trauma. There was no ecchymosis or bruising on the right shoulder exam. On his musculoskeletal exam, there was pain to palpation of the posterior shoulder at the acromioclavicular joint. There was decreased abduction and adduction to about ninety degrees only. There was there is a he has pain with supination and pronation of the right arm. There was a palpable radial radial artery pulse.\nThe nurse did a shoulder x-ray on the patient before the doctor came in the room. The right shoulder x-ray showed that the patient has an acute acromioclavicular joint fracture. The doctor came in and told the patient that he has a a a small fracture of the acromion. The doctor stated that it it\'s gon na be nothing that we need to do surgery for. The doctor stated that he wants to go ahead and just put the patient in a sling for now. The doctor stated that he wants to go ahead and prescribe meloxicam fifteen milligrams once a day. The doctor stated that he wants to go ahead and just order a cat scan of the right shoulder just to kinda get a better look of how the joint looks. The doctor stated that he\'s going to go ahead and refer the patient to physical therapy to strengthen the shoulder in hopes of avoiding surgery in the future. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight shoulder injury.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nMatthew Allen is a 67-year-old male, right-hand-dominant, male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right shoulder injury. The patient sustained this injury 4 days ago, when he was tackled a couple of times playing pick up football and landed on his right shoulder. He states that he was able to return to the game, however he has had pain since that time. The patient locates his pain to the posterior aspect of his shoulder. He denies any numbness or tingling in his fingers. He states that the strength in his hand is okay; however, he has been trying to avoid lifting anything too heavy. Mr. Allen denies being able to touch his shoulder to his ear because of pain. The patient has been taking Tylenol for pain, which does not provide relief. He denies any neck problems or other joint problems. The patient denies any previous orthopedic injuries to his right shoulder. He denies any fever or chills since the injury, but does note a headache. The patient states that he hit his head at the time of the injury but did not pass out.\n\nSURGICAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient has a history of an appendectomy 3 to 4 years ago.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nConstitutional: Denies fever or chills.\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right shoulder pain. Denies neck pain.\nNeurological: Reports headache. Denies numbness or tingling.\n\nVITALS\n\nBP: 122/76.\nHR: 82 bpm.\nSpO2: 95% on room air.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nNECK: Examination of the cervical spine reveals no tenderness to palpation. Full ROM.\nHEAD: There does not appear to be any trauma. No ecchymosis or bruising.\nMSK: Examination of the right shoulder reveals pain to palpation of the posterior shoulder at the acromioclavicular joint. There is decreased abduction and adduction to about 90 degrees only. Pain with supination and pronation of the right arm. Palpable radial artery pulse.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-ray of the right shoulder reveals an acute acromioclavicular joint fracture.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight shoulder acute acromioclavicular joint fracture.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have explained to him that his x-rays revealed an acute acromioclavicular joint fracture. The plan is to place the patient in a sling and prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once a day to treat his pain. I also recommend that we obtain a CT scan of the right shoulder to evaluate further. I recommend that the patient attend formal physical therapy to strengthen his right shoulder in hopes of avoiding surgery in the future. In the meantime, I have advised him to avoid tackle football.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
