ts hemoptysis and moderate shortness of breath.\n- **Cardiovascular:** No chest pain or palpitations.\n- **Gastrointestinal:** No nausea or vomiting.\n- **Neurological:** No headaches or dizziness.\n- **Endocrine:** Reports blood sugars have been high and low, currently on metformin.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Reports knee pain, no leg swelling.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: 120/70 mmHg\n- Heart Rate: 80 bpm\n- Respiratory Rate: 18 breaths per minute\n- Temperature: Afebrile\n- Pulse Oxygenation: 94% on pulse oximetry in the clinic\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- **General:** Alert, in mild respiratory distress.\n- **HEENT:** No JVD, no carotid bruits.\n- **Respiratory:** Rales and rhonchi heard more on the right side, diminished breath sounds in the right lung field.\n- **Cardiovascular:** 2/1 holosystolic ejection murmur, unchanged from previous exams, regular rate and rhythm.\n- **Abdomen:** Soft, non-tender, no organomegaly.\n- **Extremities:** 1+ non-pitting edema in both lower extremities, no calf tenderness, negative Homan\'s sign.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n1. **Shortness of breath with hemoptysis:**\n   - Differential Diagnosis: Possible pulmonary embolism, pneumonia, or malignancy.\n   - Awaiting results from emergency department evaluation and chest CT scan.\n\n2. **Type 2 Diabetes:**\n   - Hemoglobin A1c: 7.0%\n   - Blood sugars have been high post-meals and low in the morning.\n\n3. **Hypertension:**\n   - Blood pressure within normal limits but history of hypertension.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n1. **Shortness of breath with hemoptysis:**\n   - Referral to Emergency Department for further evaluation and imaging.\n   - Order a Chest CT scan to rule out pulmonary embolism or other pathology.\n   - Blood work to be done at the emergency department including CBC, BMP, D-dimer, and ABG.\n   - Follow-up with pulmonologist, Dr. Sarah Thompson, for further evaluation and management.\n\n2. **Type 2 Diabetes:**\n   - Order Hemoglobin A1c today.\