 it it doesn\'t feel normal. He had a marked limp on his looks like it\'s his right leg upon ambulation. Upon physical exam, we noticed a significant effusion in the right knee just a slight bit of ecchymosis. He felt some pain to palpation at the medial joint line. He felt some difficulty with passive range of motion. He lacked a few degrees of terminal extension to about a hundred and fifteen degrees as well as lacked about maybe twenty to thirty degrees of terminal flexion on the secondary to the pain. He had a positive lachman exam. We also evaluated the integrity of his toes and he had a muscle strength of a five out of five. Neurovascularly his bilateral lower extremities were intact. The right knee injury was really suggestive of anterior cruciate ligament tear. The plan was to go ahead and order an mri because we wanted to evaluate the integrity of that acl. He was told that he may need surgery but we would first take a look at that mri result and reevaluate and reevaluate that first. He was also told to go ahead and take anti-inflammatory medication, meloxicam fifty milligrams a day, and it\'s gon na help reduce that swelling and the pain. He was also told to ice the knee if he needed to if he was still seeing some swelling and some pain. He was told to moderate his activities so he would wear a knee immobilizer and then also use crutches when he would walk around just to help him a little bit so not to put too much stress and strain on that knee. He was also told that he would need to limit his work activities because he sits behind a desk but it\'s not been very comfortable sitting here lately. He was also told to do slight activities not enough to stress his legs but definitely move move them a little bit every now and then so it doesn\'t lock up on him. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee injury.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nScott Miller is a pleasant 36-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right knee injury. The injury occurred 3 days ago, when he was playing pickup soccer games and felt a pop in his right knee. He reports swelling at the time of the injury, which has improved. The patient states that his knee is not as functional or moving as well as it used to be. He rates his pain level as a 6 to 7 out of 10 currently and a 4 to 5 out of 10 when the swelling decreases. The patient has been taking ibuprofen on a regular basis, which provides some relief. He states that he is able to bend his knee with pain towards the end of this motion; however, when he goes to straighten his knee, he feels like he has to help it straight. The patient adds that he is able to bear weight on his right knee, however it feels loose and does not feel normal.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient works at a desk.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nGAIT: Marked limp in the right leg upon ambulation.\nMSK: Examination of the right knee: Significant effusion. Slight ecchymosis. Pain with palpation at the medial joint line. Difficulty with passive ROM. Lacking a few degrees of terminal extension to 115 degrees. Lacking 20-30 degrees of terminal flexion secondary to pain. Positive Lachman\'s test. Muscle strength is 5/5. Neurovascular intact distally.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee pain, possible anterior cruciate ligament tear.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have recommended that we obtain an MRI of the right knee to evaluate the integrity of the ACL. I have prescribed the patient meloxicam 15 mg a day to reduce swelling and pain. We discussed that the patient should utilize ice and activity modification to reduce swelling and pain. I recommended that he wears a knee immobilizer and use crutches when he is ambulating. He will avoid jogging until we have the results of his MRI.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me once the MRI results are available for review and further discussion.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
