and that it just means it\'s pain caused from overuse and damage to the tendons in your arm what we are gon na do is rest it i\'m gon na order a sling for your right arm and you can wear this while you\'re awake well i want you to apply ice to your elbow for twenty minutes three times a day i\'m going to i\'m gon na ask you to take six hundred milligrams of ibuprofen that\'s three pills every six hours with food and you can take that for one week i know it\'s really important that you said that you have something that a gift that you wan na make for your son but we\'re gon na hold off on pottery for the next couple of weeks just to give your arm a chance to heal you know like it\'s awesome that you wan na be able to do these things for your family but sometimes you know your body is not where your where your where your heart is like where your heart and your head are right and then so\n[patient] i\'ll i\'ll just let him know that that i maybe i\'ll take a picture of what i have so far\n[doctor] absolutely that\'s awesome and so it\'ll be like a a movie like coming soon to a shelf near you that\'s great for your second problem right we have this longstanding diabetes i want to increase your metformin to a thousand milligrams twice a day i know that you\'re saying you\'re having a hard time keeping up with that so i want you to do a good job of like checking and recording your blood sugars every day i do need you to follow up with me in a couple weeks i also wan na order something called an a1c since you are having a hard time with like the blood like the blood sugars everyday an a1c will give me a more accurate picture of like a longer period of time and then we might need to evaluate like what are what other options that we have if your sugars if we ca n\'t get them a bit more under control i do want you to follow up with me in a couple of weeks about the about your diabetes and for your third problem of hypertension we\'re just gon na continue you on your lisinopril at ten milligrams that\'s it seems to be doing it\'s job i\'m really i\'m really glad about that and it\'s probably due to the walking that you\'re doing i forgot to mention for your diabetes we are gon na i\'m gon na recommend a a referral to diabetic counseling that way you can get like a bit more information on like the steps that you can take to help you with that\n[patient] i was gon na ask you how about my x-ray the x-ray of my elbow how did that look\n[doctor] your x-ray honestly looks great in that i do n\'t see any kind of fracture or bony abnormality so that\'s what makes me think that this is like a tendon related thing right so in reviewing your x-ray like it it does support the the the assessment and plan that i have given you i\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] do you\n[patient] just true\n[doctor] yeah absolutely i do think for your your primary problem the elbow that we might wan na get put you in some physical rehab if the problem is taking a little longer to heal but we\'ll discuss that at your next visit here okay\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] alright no problem any other questions\n[patient] nope\n[doctor] alright thank you for keeping me accountable\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ #####\n**1. Subjective:**\n\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\nElbow pain.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nTeresa Johnson is a 55-year-old African American female who presents to the urgent care center with complaints of moderate to severe pain in her right elbow. The pain started 4 days ago after an episode of intense activity while working on a pottery project. She describes the pain as a 7/10 in severity, constant, and it radiates down her arm. The pain is associated with swelling and tenderness along the medial aspect of the elbow. The pain has significantly impacted her ability to perform her pottery work and other daily activities that involve repetitive wrist movements. She denies any trauma to the elbow prior to the onset of symptoms.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Reports moderate to severe pain in the right elbow, radiating down the arm, associated with swelling and tenderness.\n- **General:** Poor sleep due to pain.\n- **Endocrine:** Known history of hypertension, currently managed with 10 mg of lisinopril daily.\n- **Endocrine:** Known history of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, currently managed with 500 mg of metformin BID.\n- **Dietary:** High carbohydrate intake, sweet tooth, enjoys baking and pottery.\n- **Exercise:** Walks 1-2 times a week, 30 minutes each session.\n- **Tobacco Use:** None.\n- **Alcohol Consumption:** Occasional social drinking.\n- **Drug Use:** None.\n- **Recreational Activities:** Enjoys pottery and baking.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: 122/70 mmHg\n- Heart Rate: 78 bpm\n- Respiratory Rate: 18 breaths per minute\n- Temperature: 97.9°F\n- SpO2: 98% on room air\n\n**Physical Exam:**\n- **General:** Alert, cooperative, appears in mild distress due to pain.\n- **Cardiovascular:** No murmur, rub, or gallop. Pulses are equal bilaterally.\n- **Respiratory:** Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Right elbow: Moderate tenderness over the medial epicondyle, pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm, no deformity noted. Pain with flexion and wrist extension.\n- **Integumentary:** No rash, bruising, or swelling noted at the elbow.\n- **Endocrine:** No acanthosis nigricans or thyroid enlargement.\n\n**Investigations:**\n- X-ray of the right elbow: No fractures or bony abnormalities noted.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n1. **Primary Diagnosis:**\n   - Right elbow pain, consistent with medial epicondylitis (tennis elbow).\n   - Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s symptoms and physical examination findings are consistent with medial epicondylitis. X-ray of the elbow showed no fractures or bony abnormalities, supporting the diagnosis.\n\n2. **Secondary Diagnosis:**\n   - Longstanding Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus.\n   - Medical Reasoning: The patient has a known history of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, currently managed with metformin. The patient reports difficulty maintaining blood sugar levels and has been non-compliant with medication.\n\n3. **Third Diagnosis:**\n   - Hypertension.\n   - Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of hypertension, currently managed with lisinopril, with blood pressure within an acceptable range today.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n1. **Right Elbow Pain:**\n   - **Rest:** Advise the patient to rest the right arm and avoid activities that exacerbate the pain.\n   - **Immobilization:** Recommend the use of a sling for the right arm while awake.\n   - **Ice Application:** Advise the patient to apply ice to the elbow for 20 minutes three times a day.\n   - **Medication:** Prescribe 600 mg of ibuprofen, to be taken orally three times a day with food for 1 week.\n   - **Follow-up:** Schedule a follow-up visit in 2 weeks to assess improvement and consider physical rehabilitation if necessary.\n\n2. **Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus:**\n   - **Medication Adjustment:** Increase metformin to 1000 mg BID.\n   - **Blood Sugar Monitoring:** Encourage the patient to check blood sugars daily and record the results.\n   - **Education:** Provide education on the importance of blood sugar control and adherence to medication.\n   - **Follow-up:** Schedule a follow-up visit in 2 weeks to review blood sugar logs and consider further management if no improvement.\n\n3. **Hypertension:**\n   - **Continue Current Management:** Continue lisinopril 10 mg daily.\n   - **Monitoring:** Regularly monitor blood pressure at home.\n   - **Lifestyle Modifications:** Encourage continued walking and discuss potential benefits of increased physical activity within the patient\'s pain-free range.\n\n4. **Additional Recommendations:**\n   - **Referral:** Refer the patient to Dr. Emily Carter, a dietician, for diabetic counseling due to difficulty maintaining blood sugar levels.\n   - **Imaging:** Order a biochemistry panel including HbA1c to assess long-term glucose control.\n\n**Patient Agreements:**\nThe patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n**Instructions:**\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks for re-evaluation of the elbow and to review blood sugar logs.\n##### }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ We will be discussing the case of a 57-year-old female who presented to the office with a chief complaint of right elbow pain. She was very excited to share with the doctor that she was in the middle of a pottery project and was creating a ceramic bowl for her son\'s birthday. She was also planning on painting the bowl and adding real full decorations. She stated that she had been working on this project a lot and her arm had started hurting so much that she couldn\'t work on it anymore. She reported pain on the inside of her elbow that radiated down her arm and along the medial aspect of her arm. She denied any direct trauma to the elbow and stated that she had never injured her elbow before. On a scale of one to ten with ten being her arm is actively being sought off by a chainsaw, she reported her pain to be a seven. She stated that it kept her up at night and if she would roll over in bed it would wake her up. She stated that she had taken some ibuprofen but that was not relieving the pain. She reported that the pain had started four days ago and was not getting better. She also stated that she had a history of hypertension and was on ten milligrams of lisinopril. She reported that her blood pressures were usually one teens to one thirties. She stated that she had been compliant with her medications and would set an alarm to remind herself to take them. She was also being treated for diabetes and was on five hundred milligrams of metformin twice a day. She stated that she would forget to take her medications at times and would admit that she had a sweet tooth. She would bake cakes and senna rules and had been doing so since she was very young. She stated that she had gotten the recipe for simpon rolls out of a southern living magazine a long time ago.\nThe doctor then began the physical exam and asked the patient to extend her arm and show her where it hurts. The patient showed the doctor that it hurts on the inside of her arm. The doctor then asked if it hurts when she does certain things and the patient stated that it hurts with flexion and pronation of the forearm. The doctor then checked her vital signs and stated that her blood pressure was one twenty over seventy which was a great improvement. The doctor stated that she was satting one hundred percent on room air and that her respiratory rate was eighteen. She was afebrile with a temperature of ninety seven point nine. The doctor stated that she had a five pound increase in weight from the last time she was in the office but that was completely normal to fluctuate. The doctor stated that it was great that she was getting out and walking daily and that she was doing a good job of managing her hypertension. The doctor stated that she was going to order a sling for her right arm and that she should apply ice to her elbow for twenty minutes three times a day. The doctor stated that she should take six hundred milligrams of ibuprofen every six hours with food and that she should do this for one week. The doctor stated that she should hold off on pottery for the next couple of weeks and that she should follow up in a couple of weeks. The doctor stated that she should increase her metformin to a thousand milligrams twice a day and that she should follow up with her in a couple of weeks. The doctor stated that she should follow up with her in a couple of weeks about her diabetes and that she should check her blood sugars every day. The doctor stated that if her sugars were not under control that they would evaluate other options. The doctor stated that she was going to refer her to diabetic counseling and that it was due to the walking