sculoskeletal:** Reports moderate to severe pain in the right elbow, radiating down the arm, associated with swelling and tenderness.\n- **General:** Poor sleep due to pain.\n- **Endocrine:** Known history of hypertension, currently managed with 10 mg of lisinopril daily.\n- **Endocrine:** Known history of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, currently managed with 500 mg of metformin BID.\n- **Dietary:** High carbohydrate intake, sweet tooth, enjoys baking and pottery.\n- **Exercise:** Walks 1-2 times a week, 30 minutes each session.\n- **Tobacco Use:** None.\n- **Alcohol Consumption:** Occasional social drinking.\n- **Drug Use:** None.\n- **Recreational Activities:** Enjoys pottery and baking.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: 122/70 mmHg\n- Heart Rate: 78 bpm\n- Respiratory Rate: 18 breaths per minute\n- Temperature: 97.9°F\n- SpO2: 98% on room air\n\n**Physical Exam:**\n- **General:** Alert, cooperative, appears in mild distress due to pain.\n- **Cardiovascular:** No murmur, rub, or gallop. Pulses are equal bilaterally.\n- **Respiratory:** Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Right elbow: Moderate tenderness over the medial epicondyle, pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm, no deformity noted. Pain with flexion and wrist extension.\n- **Integumentary:** No rash, bruising, or swelling noted at the elbow.\n- **Endocrine:** No acanthosis nigricans or thyroid enlargement.\n\n**Investigations:**\n- X-ray of the right elbow: No fractures or bony abnormalities noted.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n1. **Primary Diagnosis:**\n   - Right elbow pain, consistent with medial epicondylitis (tennis elbow).\n   - Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s symptoms and physical examination findings are consistent with medial epicondylitis. X-ray of the elbow showed no fractures or bony abnormalities, supporting the diagnosis.\n\n2. **Secondary Diagnosis:**\n   - Longstanding Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus.\n   - Medical Reasoning: The patient has a known history of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, currently managed with metformin. The patient reports difficulty maintaining blood sugar levels and has been non-compliant with medication.\n\n3. **Third Diagnosis:**\n   - Hypertension.\n   - Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of hypertension, currently managed with lisinopril, with blood pressure within an acceptable range today.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n1. **Right Elbow Pain:**\n   - **Rest:** Advise the patient to rest the right arm and avoid activities that exacerbate the pain.\n   - **Immobilization:** Recommend the use of a sling for the right arm while awake.\n   - **Ice Application:** Advise the patient to apply ice to the elbow for 20 minutes three times a day.\n   - **Medication:** Prescribe 600 mg of ibuprofen, to be taken orally three times a day with food for 1 week.\n   - **Follow-up:** Schedule a follow-up visit in 2 weeks to assess improvement and consider physical rehabilitation if necessary.\n\n2. **Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus:**\n   - **Medication Adjustment:** Increase metformin to 1000 mg BID.\n   - **Blood Sugar Monitoring:** Encourage the patient to check blood sugars daily and record the results.\n   - **Education:** Provide education on the importance of blood sugar control and adherence to medication.\n   - **Follow-up:** Schedule a follow-up visit in 2 weeks to review blood sugar logs and consider further management if no improvement.\n\n3. **Hypertension:**\n   - **Continue Current Management:** Continue lisinopril 10 mg daily.\n   - **Monitoring:** Regularly monitor blood pressure at home.\n   - **Lifestyle Modifications:** Encourage continued walking and discuss potential benefits of increased physical activity within the patient\'s pain-free range.\n\n4. **Additional Recommendations:**\n   - **Referral:** Refer the patient to Dr. Emily Carter, a dietician, for diabetic counseling due to difficulty maintaining blood sugar levels.\n   - **Imaging:** Order a biochemistry panel including HbA1c to assess long-term glucose control.\n\n**Patient Agreements:**\nThe patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n**Instructions:**\nThe patient will follow up in 2 weeks for re-evaluation of the elbow and to review blood sugar logs.\n##### }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ We will be discussing the case of a 57-year-old female who presented to the office with a chief complaint of right elbow pain. She was very excited to share with the doctor that she was in the middle of a pottery project and was creating a ceramic bowl for her son\'s birthday. She was also planning on painting the bowl and adding real full decorations. She stated that she had been working on this project a lot and her arm had started hurting so much that she couldn\'t work on it anymore. She reported pain on the inside of her elbow that radiated down her arm and along the medial aspect of her arm. She denied any direct trauma to the elbow and stated that she had never injured her elbow before. On a scale of one to ten with ten being her arm is actively being sought off by a chainsaw, she reported her pain to be a seven. She stated that it kept her up at night and if she would roll over in bed it would wake her up. She stated that she had taken some ibuprofen but that was not relieving the pain. She reported that the pain had started four days ago and was not getting better. She also stated that she had a history of hypertension and was on ten milligrams of lisinopril. She reported that her blood pressures were usually one teens to one thirties. She stated that she had been compliant with her medications and would set an alarm to remind herself to take them. She was also being treated for diabetes and was on five hundred milligrams of metformin twice a day. She stated that she would forget to take her medications at times and would admit that she had a sweet tooth. She would bake cakes and senna rules and had been doing so since she was very young. She stated that she had gotten the recipe for simpon rolls out of a southern living magazine a long time ago.\nThe doctor then began the physical exam and asked the patient to extend her arm and show her where it hurts. The patient showed the doctor that it hurts on the inside of her arm. The doctor then asked if it hurts when she does certain things and the patient stated that it hurts with flexion and pronation of the forearm. The doctor then checked her vital signs and stated that her blood pressure was one twenty over seventy which was a great improvement. The doctor stated that she was satting one hundred percent on room air and that her respiratory rate was eighteen. She was afebrile with a temperature of ninety seven point nine. The doctor stated that she had a five pound increase in weight from the last time she was in the office but that was completely normal to fluctuate. The doctor stated that it was great that she was getting out and walking daily and that she was doing a good job of managing her hypertension. The doctor stated that she was going to order a sling for her right arm and that she should apply ice to her elbow for twenty minutes three times a day. The doctor stated that she should take six hundred milligrams of ibuprofen every six hours with food and that she should do this for one week. The doctor stated that she should hold off on pottery for the next couple of weeks and that she should follow up in a couple of weeks. The doctor stated that she should increase her metformin to a thousand milligrams twice a day and that she should follow up with her in a couple of weeks. The doctor stated that she should follow up with her in a couple of weeks about her diabetes and that she should check her blood sugars every day. The doctor stated that if her sugars were not under control that they would evaluate other options. The doctor stated that she was going to refer her to diabetic counseling and that it was due to the walking that she was doing. The doctor stated that the x-ray of her elbow was great and that there was no fracture or bony abnormality. The doctor stated that this was consistent with medial epicondylitis and that it was pain caused from overuse and damage to the tendons in her arm. The doctor stated that they would hold off on the x }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight elbow pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nTeresa Sullivan is a pleasant 33-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right elbow pain. Her pain began 4 days ago, without any improvement. She denies any specific injury; however, she has recently increased her usage of a pottery wheel in creating a bowl for her son. She is currently unable to work with the pottery wheel secondary to pain. Her pain is in the medial aspect of her elbow. She notes it radiates medially down her arm as well. The patient rates her pain level as 7 out of 10 at its worst. She reports the pain wakes her at night if she rolls over. She denies ibuprofen or ice provide pain relief. The patient denies any previous elbow injuries.\n\nThe patient has a history of hypertension. She states she has been checking her blood pressures regularly. She adds that her blood pressures have been in the 110s to 130s range. She affirms she is taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and denies missing doses.\n\nRegarding her diabetes type 2, she reports she sometimes forgets to take metformin 500 mg twice daily. At times, her blood sugar levels range from 150 to 170 mg/dL. She states that she has a sweet tooth and indulges in home baked goods. For exercise, she walks in the evening and in the morning. She walks 1 to 2 miles per day around her neighborhood.\n\nThe patient enjoys working with ceramics as well as baking cakes and cinnamon rolls for family gatherings.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of hypertension and diabetes type 2.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking lisinopril 10 mg and metformin 500 mg twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right elbow pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nBlood Pressure: 120/70 mmHg.\nPulse Rate: Appropriate.\nRespiratory Rate: 18 breaths per minute.\nOxygen Saturation: 100% on room air.\nBody Temperature: 97.9 degrees F.\nPain Level: 7/10 at its worst.\nWeight: 5 lb increase since last visit.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: No murmurs, gallops or rubs. Pulses are equal bilaterally.\nRESPIRATORY: Normal respiratory effort, no respiratory distress. Lungs are clear bilaterally to auscultation.\nSKIN: No rash or lesions.\nMSK: Examination of the right elbow: Moderate tenderness at the medial epicondyle. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. Pain with flexion and bending the right wrist. No bruising or swelling. No discoloration or lesions.\n\nRESULTS\n\nAn x-ray of the right elbow was reviewed today. It demonstrated no evidence of any fractures. No other abnormalities are noted.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\n1. Right elbow pain, consistent with medial epicondylitis.\n2. Diabetes type 2.\n3. Hypertension.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with her regarding her current symptoms. I have explained to her that her elbow pain is consistent with medial epicondylitis and was most likely caused by overuse and damage to the tendons in her arm. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The patient will wear a sling on her right arm while she is awake to provide increased support. I advised her to ice her right elbow for 20 minutes 3 times per day. She will take ibuprofen 600 mg every 6 hours with food for 1 week. I have also advised her to hold off on creating pottery for the next 2 weeks to allow her arm to heal. If the patient continues to experience persistent elbow pain, we may consider formal physical therapy at her next visit.\n\nI have encouraged the patient to check and record her blood sugars every day. I will increase her metformin to 1000 mg twice a day. The patient and I discussed the importance of her taking her medication on a regular basis twice a day. I have also recommended that she be referred to diabetic counseling for more information on the steps she can take to manage her diabetes. I will also order a hemoglobin A1c test for a more accurate assessment of her long-term blood sugar levels.\n\nThe patient’s hypertension is currently controlled with medication and exercise. I have instructed her to continue taking lisinopril 10 mg. I also encouraged her to continue with her walking routine.\n\nAll questions were answered.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to review the results of the hemoglobin A1c test and discuss her progress.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
