d the pain continues i\'m gon na go ahead and maybe order a cat scan and then at that point in time we can refer you to a pain doctor if we\'re still having significant pain\n[patient] sounds good\n[doctor] okay thank you i\'m gon na send my nursing to see you and we\'ll get you out the door\n[patient] thank you\n[doctor] take care\n[patient] that one is a follow-up mm-hmm\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ #####\n**1. Subjective:**\n\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\nLow back pain.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nNoah Lee is a 35-year-old Asian male with a past medical history significant for Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, presenting with moderate low back pain for the past week. The pain has been continuous and has not responded to over-the-counter medications. The patient reports that the pain is localized in the middle of the back and radiates to the left side. The pain is aggravated by sudden movements, particularly on the left side.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Endorses moderate low back pain, continuous, radiating to the left side, aggravated by sudden movements.\n- **Endocrine:** History of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, controlled with medication.\n- **Neurological:** No reported numbness or tingling in the lower extremities.\n- **General:** No recent weight loss or fever.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- **General:** Alert and oriented, in mild distress due to pain.\n- **HEENT:** Normocephalic, atraumatic, no scleral icterus.\n- **Neck:** Supple, no lymphadenopathy.\n- **Respiratory:** Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n- **Cardiovascular:** Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs.\n- **Musculoskeletal:** Tenderness to palpation in the lumbosacral region, particularly in the middle of the back and radiating to the left side. No costovertebral angle tenderness. Full range of motion in the lower extremities, though pain is elicited with certain movements.\n- **Neurological:** Strength 5/5 in lower extremities, sensation intact, reflexes 2+ and symmetric.\n- **Endocrine:** No thyromegaly, no acanthosis nigricans.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n- **Low back pain, unspecified (ICD-10 M54.50).**\n- **Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, controlled.**\n\n**4. Plan:**\n1. **Low Back Pain:**\n   - **Medical Reasoning:** The patient presents with moderate low back pain with a one-week duration, aggravated by sudden movements and radiating to the left side. Physical examination reveals tenderness in the lumbosacral region.\n   - **Patient Education and Counseling:** Discussed the importance of rest, gentle movements, and avoiding activities that exacerbate the pain. Recommended using over-the-counter pain relief as needed.\n   - **Medical Treatment:** Ordered physical therapy for two sessions per week for two weeks to help with pain management and strengthening of the back muscles.\n   - **Imaging:** Ordered a low back X-ray to evaluate joint spaces and check for any disk issues. Awaiting results.\n   - **Follow-up:** Advised to follow up in one week to reassess pain and review X-ray results.\n\n2. **Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus:**\n   - **Medical Reasoning:** The patient has a known history of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus, currently controlled with metformin.\n   - **Patient Education and Counseling:** Discussed the importance of maintaining a balanced diet and regular exercise. Advised to avoid high carbohydrate intake, especially from frequent visits to the bakery.\n   - **Medical Treatment:** Continue current medication regimen of metformin 500 mg twice daily. Ordered an A1C test to evaluate current glycemic control. Awaiting results.\n   - **Follow-up:** Advised to monitor blood sugar levels regularly and follow up in one month for A1C results and further management.\n\n**Patient Agreements:**\nThe patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n**Instructions:**\nThe patient will follow up in one week for reassessment of pain and review of X-ray results. Continue with prescribed medications and lifestyle modifications. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ We present a case of a 44-year-old male, no known allergies, with a history of diabetes mellitus, last follow-up in the office five months ago, who presents to the ED with complaints of low back pain, started one week ago, which was not relieved with over the counter medications. He states that he can’t remember if he injured himself, but he recalls that he was twisting to the left and maybe picked something up. At that time, there was pain, but he thought that was the end of it, but after a few hours, it was still there. Since then, it’s been there. It’s more towards the middle of the back, maybe on the lower side and nothing on the you know i mean it sometimes it goes back you know maybe having troubles with the left eye i suppose but i mean it’s aggravated you know when i you know when i do some you know maybe i’m a sudden movement to the left. He states that he gets the pain more in the center but it goes across to the little back and i on the left side and he also heard that if he twists suddenly that makes it worse. He’s on metformin twice a day and he tries to still have sugar but you know i mean he you know you know i mean whenever he gets the chance i mean he you know i exercise but you know i mean that’s about it. He’s been okay with his diabetes and he’s been taking his meds.\nOn exam, he has no nuchal rigidity, no Kernig’s sign, no Brudzinski’s sign, and his muscle strength is 5/5 in both upper and lower extremities. He has a positive straight leg raising test on the left side. He has no pain or numbness or tingling in the feet. He has a positive psoas sign. The rest of the neurological exam is unremarkable. He has a moderate tenderness to palpation in the lower back, more towards the left side. The rest of the musculoskeletal exam is unremarkable. He has no gritting of the face, no midline tenderness, no upper }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBack pain.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of diabetes.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient\'s family owns and runs a bakery.\n\nMEDICATIONS\n\nPatient reports taking metformin twice daily.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports back pain.\nNeurological: Denies numbness or tingling in feet.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nEyes\n- Examination: No redness, discharge, or icterus.\n\nEars, Nose, Mouth, and Throat\n- Examination of Ears: Auditory canal and tympanic membranes are clear.\n\nRespiratory\n- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.\n\nCardiovascular\n- Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm.\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: No pain to palpation of the upper back. Pain to palpation of the midline of the lower back, especially on the left. No pain to palpation on the right lower back. Mildly positive straight leg rise on the right Positive straight leg rise on the left.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Low back pain.\n- Medical Reasoning: I believe he has a musculoskeletal low back strain. I do not expect to see anything significant on x-ray, but we will obtain one for further evaluation of any possible disc issues.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed his diagnosis at length today. I explained that physical therapy can help with the pain, but also strengthen some of the muscles in the back. I explained that he needs to be careful while taking medications, such as an overuse of muscle relaxers if he is doing any strenuous or hazardous activities such as driving.\n- Medical Treatment: Low back x-ray ordered. Referral to physical therapy provided. He will take 400 mg of ibuprofen up to 3 times per day. Tylenol can be used for severe pain. Prescription for Flexeril also provided.\n\n2. Diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: His diabetes is currently under control.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: We discussed the importance of maintaining a healthy lifestyle. I encouraged him to try an avoid foods high in sugar. He asked many questions and I feel he is confident in the plan at this point.\n- Medical Treatment: Hemoglobin A1c ordered as he has not had an updated one in almost 5 months. He will continue with his diet and exercise. Continue current dose of metformin for the time being. The patient will be checking his blood sugar in the morning and will log this information for me.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], request_timeout=60)[0m


