tion of the right lateral knee, full range of motion with no instability, positive varus test, negative Lachman sign.\n- Cardiovascular: Palpable dorsalis pedis and posterior tibialis pulse.\n- Neurological: No focal deficits.\n\n**Investigation/Test Results:**\n- X-ray of the right knee: No acute fracture or bony abnormality.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n- Sprain of the lateral collateral ligament of the right knee.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n- Prescribed meloxicam 15 mg orally once daily for pain management.\n- Advised physical therapy to strengthen the surrounding muscles and improve knee stability.\n- Recommended icing the knee 20 minutes on, 20 minutes off, 4 times a day.\n- Instructed to avoid high-impact activities such as burpees and to use supportive footwear.\n- Follow-up in 4 weeks for re-evaluation.\n- Referral to physical therapy for targeted rehabilitation exercises.\n- MRI of the right knee if symptoms persist post-physical therapy to evaluate for any ligament damage or other structural issues.\n\nThe patient was cooperative and expressed understanding of the treatment plan. He has a high health literacy and is motivated to recover quickly to resume his active lifestyle, including regular cycling and Peloton workouts. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Gregory is a 49-year-old male who presented to the office after injuring his right knee stepping off a curb. He reported that he was not running, but rather he turned over his ankle to the outside and thought his ankle was the thing that was going to hurt most, but after the ankle soreness went away the only his knee hurt and it sort of clicked when he would walk. It didn\'t hurt when he would walk, unless he stood on one foot for an extended period of time, like leaning his foot up against the wall. He would feel like it was locked and then he would sort of have to ease into walking again. He took ibuprofen but wasn\'t a big pill taker, so he sort of sucked it up and didn\'t really take it seriously. At night it did