 way up into his calf. He was an aggressive triathlon trainee and was running approximately ten klometers, trying to get on the bicycle for about forty-five minutes to an hour in the morning at lunchtime, and then in the evening he would jump in the pool and swim for about another thirty minutes to an hour. He was doing this five days a week on the weekends, he kinda alternated between you know a long run and a long bike don\'t really do a long swim but he was swimming on those other five days and he was taking one day off. On his running days or when he does the running he was not an aggressive stretcher, he does not stretch at all, he does not have time for that. He does not stretch at all. With the bicycle, he does not really work the lower legs to get them stretched out, he just jumps on and goes. When he first starts off he goes slow until he\'s warmed up and then he goes faster. He told me that when he first gets out of bed it\'s just it just feels really tight and almost like it\'s tearing and after he been walking around for about ten or fifteen minutes it eases up a little bit but if if he ever sits down for any extended period of time and then tries to get back up it\'s really sore again. Or after a lot of activity it\'ll get sore too. I\'m sure this is impacting that ability to to really focus on his training uh as he\'s going with that with that type of a pain so I\'m just going to go ahead and let\'s let\'s take a look at his his and his lower extremities here and yeah okay so he rolls up his pants yeah okay good now does his left leg hurt at all does he get that severe pain or is it mainly on the right? No it\'s mainly just on the right. I\'m just going to hold his leg here and I want him to take his his foot and ankle and I want him to go ahead and just pull his his toes up towards him does that create some pain when he does that? A little bit. A little bit. Okay he rolls over here on my stretcher for me and I want him to dangle his his feet over the edge and what I wan na do is I just wan na I\'m gon na squeeze his left calf first and when I do that does that hurt on his left side? No. No. I\'m gon na squeeze on the right side anything there? No. Okay okay well that\'s good his his thompson test is negative that\'s a really good finding and I just wan na continue pressing here on his right from his heel when I squeeze his heel does that cause significant pain at all? Yeah. Yeah okay and if I come up a little bit further as I i run up that achilles tendon does that hurt? That\'s real tender. Yeah okay great not great so yeah go ahead and sits up for me so dennis my diagnosis is achilles tendinitis and it\'s really from overuse when we see this frequently when somebody starts to you know get in and train aggressively for an event I\'m unfortunately I\'m gon na have to ask him to stop training for a a week or two I know that\'s concerning but I really want this to heal before we move into that next phase I didn\'t ask him about medication but I\'m assuming whatever he was doing wasn\'t working significantly so what I\'d like him to do is take some ibuprofen or advil that\'s the same medication and I want him to take three tablets every six hours and that\'s really an anti-inflammatory see if we can eliminate some of that tendinitis type pain that he\'s getting that inflammation around that achilles tendon I\'m gon na also order a couple physical therapy visits and the reason for that is I\'d like him to get some strength and stretching understanding I think it\'s important for him especially here at the beginning is to get some of that real stretching knowledge in and learn how to stretch those muscles before he starts these activities and they are gon na help strengthen his his lower extremities also and I want him to come back to me within you know a week to ten days following his first couple physical therapy appointments so I can monitor how he\'s doing I see no reason for an x-ray at this point but if this continues we\'re gon na you know if he\'s still having pain ten days in significant pain without change modification of his activity then we may have to look at more aggressive treatment plans how does that sound for him? Sounds good. Hey did you happen to catch that pittsburgh pang one hockey game last night? No i i was watching the track meet. My gosh the the pang ones are on fire you know they beat up on new york seven to two i mean the hockey\'s just an amazing sport i i just do you guy do a lot of hockey down there in town here? Used to when i lived in detroit. Oh. No yeah detroit is a hockey town yeah the red wings okay well I\'m gon na go ahead and I\'ll send my nurse in and go ahead and get him discharged and like I said I\'d like to see him back here in seven to ten days after that first physical therapy appointment I\'ve got a great phenomenal office staff you just call in once that gets scheduled they will get you in to that next available appointment so take care and I will talk to you later. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nDennis Martin is a pleasant 46-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right heel pain. The onset of his pain began 10 days ago while training for a triathlon. He locates his pain to the posterior aspect of his heel, which radiates proximally into his calf. His pain is aggravated by working out and when he first gets up in the morning. If he sits for a long period, his pain will return upon standing. The patient states that he runs 10 kilometers in the morning. At lunchtime, he tries to get on a bike for about 45 minutes to an hour. In the evening, he goes to the aquatic center and swims for 30 minutes to 1 hour. The patient states that he runs 5 days per week. He adds that he alternates between a long run and a long bike ride on weekends. He currently takes one day off from his exercises. The patient states that he does not stretch at all. He denies any left leg pain.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right heel pain. Denies left leg pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nMSK: Examination of the right heel: Pain with resisted extension. Mild pain with flexion. Positive calcaneal squeeze test. Severe tenderness to the Achilles tendon. Thompson test is negative, bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nNo new imaging was obtained today\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight Achilles tendinitis.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination today, I had a lengthy discussion with the patient regarding his current symptoms. I have explained to him that his symptoms are likely secondary to overuse. I have recommended that we treat the patient conservatively. The patient was instructed to take at least 1 week off from his training to recover from this. I will provide him with a referral for formal physical therapy to strengthen his right lower extremities. I have advised him to take 3 tablets of ibuprofen every 6 hours for pain. If his pain does not improve with physical therapy, I will recommend obtaining an x-ray.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 7 to 10 days to check on his progress.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and P