ement.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nThe patient, Ms. Julia Thompson, is a 58-year-old African American female with a history of coronary artery disease, hypertension, and type 2 diabetes mellitus, who presents for follow-up after being hospitalized for a STEMI. The patient was admitted to the hospital with severe chest pain and was promptly taken to the catheterization lab for stent placement. She was previously seen in our clinic six months ago for coronary artery disease management. She is currently asymptomatic and reports no chest pain or shortness of breath. She follows a high-sodium diet and walks her dog daily for exercise. She is compliant with her medication regimen and has good insurance coverage.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- Cardiovascular: No chest pain, no palpitations.\n- Respiratory: No shortness of breath, no cough.\n- Gastrointestinal: No nausea or vomiting.\n- Musculoskeletal: Mild lower extremity edema noted.\n- Neurological: No dizziness, no syncope.\n- Endocrine: No polyuria, no polydipsia.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: 140/85 mmHg\n- Heart Rate: 78 bpm\n- Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute\n- Temperature: 98.6°F\n- Oxygen Saturation: 98% on room air\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- General: Alert, oriented, and in no acute distress.\n- Neck: No jugular venous distention (JVD).\n- Cardiovascular: S1 and S2 present, no murmurs, no carotid bruits, 3/6 systolic ejection murmur heard at the left base.\n- Respiratory: Lungs clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n- Extremities: Mild lower extremity edema, pitting 1+.\n- Vascular: Right radial artery catheter site clean, dry, and intact without hematoma. Right radial artery pulses are palpable.\n\n**Investigation/Test Results:**\n- ECG: Normal sinus rhythm, R wave progression, ST changes consistent with prior STEMI.\n- Echocardiogram: Reduced ejection fraction (EF) of 35%, moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n**Primary Diagnosis:**\n- Coronary Artery Disease (ICD-10 I25.9)\n\n**Secondary Diagnoses:**\n- Newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction\n- Moderate mitral regurgitation\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n**Medical Treatment:**\n- Continue Aspirin 81 mg daily.\n- Continue Brilinta 90 mg twice daily.\n- Continue high-dose statin (Lipitor 80 mg daily).\n- Continue Toprol (50 mg daily).\n- Refer to cardiac rehab for exercise and education.\n- Referral to Dr. Emily Carter, a cardiologist, for further evaluation and management of reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\n**Patient Education and Counseling:**\n- Discussed the importance of continued medication adherence.\n- Advised on dietary modifications to reduce sodium intake.\n- Encouraged regular exercise as tolerated, with a structured plan to be developed in cardiac rehab.\n- Discussed the significance of follow-up and monitoring for potential complications.\n\n**Follow-Up:**\n- Return visit in 2 months for repeat echocardiogram.\n- Blood Work: Ordered for biochemistry and hematology to monitor for any abnormalities.\n- Lifestyle Modifications: Nutritional counseling to address high-sodium diet and explore diet options available within the food desert.\n\n**Patient Agreements:**\nThe patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan and is motivated to follow through with the lifestyle changes and follow-up appointments. She is compliant with her medication regimen and is eager to regain full function. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ We have a patient who is a fifty one year old female who initially presented with RCA stent placement in twenty eighteen. She came in for follow up after an interior stemi where we had to place another stent. She was very compliant with her medications and had no chest pain or shortness of breath. She had walked her dog daily for the past six months. She was very excited about fall setting in and had no plans for the holiday. She had a history of coronary artery 