over six systolic ejection murmur heard at the that i\'m hearing at the left base that\'s not like a huge deal especially considering like what you\'ve been through already in terms of the hospital but it is something that i wan na know in your chart when i listen to you your lungs your lungs are clear over here with your extremities i do notice you know some lower lower edema swelling sorry some lower swelling edema lower limb edema and it it is pitting one plus on your right radial artery the cath site is clean dry and intact without hematoma and i also your right radial artery pulses are palpable so i did review the results of your ekg and they show a normal sinus rhythm and a good r wave progression and a a evolutionary changes that are anticipated after a stemi one of the reasons though that we had you come in is that we noticed on your echo there is a reduced ejection fraction of thirty five percent we are a little bit concerned about that so let\'s talk about my assessment and plan for you your primary and like first diagnosis is gon na be coronary artery disease right you already have a history of that but you know we\'re gon na do some things to make we\'re gon na continue to evaluate that over time i want you to continue your aspirin eighty one milligrams daily and your brilinta ninety milligrams twice daily i wan na continue you on a high dose statin called lipitor and you\'re gon na be taking that eighty milligrams a day and we\'re gon na continue you on your toprol and that\'s gon na be fifty milligrams daily i am gon na refer you to cardiac rehab so we can get you some education and give you some confidence to get back to exercising regularly i know it was kinda scary to have such an episode but i promise like most of the patients i have love cardiac rehab and i think you\'ll do pretty well for your second diagnosis we have newly you have newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation what i think is like what that means is that you\'re you\'re pumping like the way that your heart is pumping is a bit concerning but i think like given your history that it will improve over time they got you into the cath lab pretty quickly so i think that the muscle is just kinda like stunned like surprised and since you\'re compliant with your meds i think you will recover we are gon na continue you on your lisinopril twenty milligrams daily however considering that you are retaining fluid i think that your third diagnosis is like we are crossing into acute heart failure i\'m gon na prescribe something called a diuretic it\'s called lasix and i do want to we need to take that forty milligrams once a day i wan na add aldactone twelve . five milligrams daily and i need you to get labs finally we will repeat another echo in about two months last but not least there is the hypertension your blood pressure seems fine i think with the aldactone that you\'ll tolerate that pretty well but we do want to maybe get you into some nutrition counseling to consider like what other diet options might be available to you maybe suggestions of things that you have n\'t thought about like cutting out meat especially any kind of preserved meat like pepperoni but maybe finding you some alternatives how does that sound\n[patient] that sounds good yeah so i\'m yeah as long as you tell me i\'m gon na get better i believe you\n[doctor] yes yeah i i strongly believe this is our best course of action and that you will get better given enough time i i see that you are making efforts i\'m really glad that you are out there walking and that you\'re cognizant of your diet and willing to make changes we will just you know monitor it over time okay\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] alright thank you\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ #####\n**1. Subjective:**\n\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\nFollow-up after recent hospitalization for acute ST elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) and stent placement.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nThe patient, Ms. Julia Thompson, is a 58-year-old African American female with a history of coronary artery disease, hypertension, and type 2 diabetes mellitus, who presents for follow-up after being hospitalized for a STEMI. The patient was admitted to the hospital with severe chest pain and was promptly taken to the catheterization lab for stent placement. She was previously seen in our clinic six months ago for coronary artery disease management. She is currently asymptomatic and reports no chest pain or shortness of breath. She follows a high-sodium diet and walks her dog daily for exercise. She is compliant with her medication regimen and has good insurance coverage.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- Cardiovascular: No chest pain, no palpitations.\n- Respiratory: No shortness of breath, no cough.\n- Gastrointestinal: No nausea or vomiting.\n- Musculoskeletal: Mild lower extremity edema noted.\n- Neurological: No dizziness, no syncope.\n- Endocrine: No polyuria, no polydipsia.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: 140/85 mmHg\n- Heart Rate: 78 bpm\n- Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute\n- Temperature: 98.6°F\n- Oxygen Saturation: 98% on room air\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- General: Alert, oriented, and in no acute distress.\n- Neck: No jugular venous distention (JVD).\n- Cardiovascular: S1 and S2 present, no murmurs, no carotid bruits, 3/6 systolic ejection murmur heard at the left base.\n- Respiratory: Lungs clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n- Extremities: Mild lower extremity edema, pitting 1+.\n- Vascular: Right radial artery catheter site clean, dry, and intact without hematoma. Right radial artery pulses are palpable.\n\n**Investigation/Test Results:**\n- ECG: Normal sinus rhythm, R wave progression, ST changes consistent with prior STEMI.\n- Echocardiogram: Reduced ejection fraction (EF) of 35%, moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n**Primary Diagnosis:**\n- Coronary Artery Disease (ICD-10 I25.9)\n\n**Secondary Diagnoses:**\n- Newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction\n- Moderate mitral regurgitation\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n**Medical Treatment:**\n- Continue Aspirin 81 mg daily.\n- Continue Brilinta 90 mg twice daily.\n- Continue high-dose statin (Lipitor 80 mg daily).\n- Continue Toprol (50 mg daily).\n- Refer to cardiac rehab for exercise and education.\n- Referral to Dr. Emily Carter, a cardiologist, for further evaluation and management of reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation.\n\n**Patient Education and Counseling:**\n- Discussed the importance of continued medication adherence.\n- Advised on dietary modifications to reduce sodium intake.\n- Encouraged regular exercise as tolerated, with a structured plan to be developed in cardiac rehab.\n- Discussed the significance of follow-up and monitoring for potential complications.\n\n**Follow-Up:**\n- Return visit in 2 months for repeat echocardiogram.\n- Blood Work: Ordered for biochemistry and hematology to monitor for any abnormalities.\n- Lifestyle Modifications: Nutritional counseling to address high-sodium diet and explore diet options available within the food desert.\n\n**Patient Agreements:**\nThe patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan and is motivated to follow through with the lifestyle changes and follow-up appointments. She is compliant with her medication regimen and is eager to regain full function. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ We have a patient who is a fifty one year old female who initially presented with RCA stent placement in twenty eighteen. She came in for follow up after an interior stemi where we had to place another stent. She was very compliant with her medications and had no chest pain or shortness of breath. She had walked her dog daily for the past six months. She was very excited about fall setting in and had no plans for the holiday. She had a history of coronary artery disease hypertension and diab