y and albuterol as needed.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- **General:** Alert and oriented, no acute distress.\n- **Cardiovascular:** Grade 3/6 systolic ejection murmur, unchanged from prior exam.\n- **Abdominal:** Mild tenderness in the epigastrium, no rebound tenderness, no guarding. Abdomen soft, no tenderness in the right lower quadrant, no significant tenderness in the right upper quadrant.\n- **Respiratory:** Mild expiratory wheezes, otherwise normal lung exam, excellent air movement.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n1. **Acute Gastritis, unspecified, without bleeding.**\n   - Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s symptoms of abdominal pain, nausea, and occasional vomiting, along with her recent increase in coffee intake and history of alcohol use, are consistent with acute gastritis. Physical exam findings support this diagnosis.\n2. **Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus.**\n   - Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s blood glucose levels are within the controlled range, and she is currently on metformin 500 mg once daily, which was increased to 500 mg twice daily in the previous visit.\n3. **Asthma.**\n   - Medical Reasoning: The patient\'s asthma is currently well-controlled on flovent 200 mcg twice a day and albuterol as needed. No recent exacerbations reported.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n1. **Acute Gastritis.**\n   - **Medical Treatment:** Prescribed Omeprazole 20 mg, oral, twice a day, 30 tablets.\n   - **Patient Education and Counseling:** Advised the patient to reduce caffeine and alcohol intake. Recommended cutting back on spicy food as well.\n   - **Follow-up:** Follow-up in 4 weeks.\n   - **Additional Testing:** Ordered urinalysis, urine pregnancy test, CBC, and CMP to rule out other possible causes of abdominal pain.\n\n2. **Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus.**\n   - **Medical Treatment:** Continue metformin 500 mg in the morning and 500 mg in the evening. Monitor blood glucose levels.\n   - **Patient Education and Counseling:** Continue dietary modifications to manage blood 