- Ice application to the right ankle.\n  - Aircast to be applied to the right ankle for stabilization.\n  - Use of crutches for the next 1-2 days, then gradually start walking as tolerated.\n- **Medication:**\n  - Ibuprofen 400 mg, oral, every 6 hours as needed for pain, quantity: 30 tablets.\n- **Follow-up:**\n  - Follow-up in 2 weeks to assess improvement.\n- **Imaging:**\n  - No additional imaging needed at this time.\n\n**Patient Education:**\n- Advised to rest and avoid putting weight on the right ankle as much as possible.\n- Instructed on the proper use of the aircast and crutches.\n- Discussed the importance of icing and elevating the ankle to reduce swelling.\n- Encouraged to continue taking NSAIDs as needed for pain management.\n- Explained that symptoms should significantly improve in the next 2 weeks and reassured that he will be able to return to playing soccer once the ankle is healed.\n\n**Patient Agreements:**\nThe patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. All questions were answered. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Gregory is a 41-year-old female who was brought to the office with right ankle pain and swelling after falling on ice. She stated that she was going out to take off the trash and that there must have been a little patch of ice or something because all she knows is that she slipped and she was on the ground and she was being really really careful. She noticed that she couldn\'t walk on it so she had a friend who was with her who was she was actually coming to visit and so she had to help her up. Last night she was you know keeping it elevated she was resting it icing it so today it feels a little better like she could put a little bit more weight on it but she\'s definitely still limping it it\'s not her normal. She stated that she took some ibuprofen and it helped her foot pain. She stated that she\'s had a lot of injury considering she does a lot of sports her favorite right now is soccer or her favorite always is soccer and she\'s actually playing for the community league now so that is really great representing the community. She stated that her kids come and watch her play and then now the oldest one is old enough to play himself so they have a leak for four and five -year-olds so he should be starting that soon too so she knows he\'s really gon na enjoy that. She stated that her husband\'s really into soccer too so they had a great soccer league of their own here in their family. She stated that she\'s been playing for a while now so she\'s had a few injuries but she\'s always been able to play through it. She stated that this time she\'s gon na have to watch it for a while though. She denied any kind of numbness in her foot at all. On physical exam her vital signs were good. On the skin of her right ankle there is ecchymosis and that\'s bruising gregory and over the lateral malleolus associated swelling. She had tenderness to palpation and anterolaterally in the soft tissue. There was no laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress and there was no bony tenderness on palpation of the foot. On neurovascular exam of her right foot her capillary refill was less than three seconds with strong dorsalis pedis pulse and her sensation was intact to light touch. She had a right ankle x-ray which showed no fracture. The assessment and plan were that her symptoms were consistent with right ankle sprain so she was given an aircast and crutches for the next one to two days and she may start walking on it as tolerated. She was also given nsaids and }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight ankle pain and swelling.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nGregory Hernandez is a pleasant 42-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right ankle pain and swelling. The onset of his pain began yesterday after he slipped and fell on the ice while taking out the trash. He states that he was unable to ambulate at the time of injury and that a friend had to help him up. He then proceeded to ice and elevate his ankle throughout the night. His pain has since improved and he is able to bear more weight on his right ankle, however he is still limping. He denies experiencing any numbness to his right foot. In addition to ice and elevation he has been taking ibuprofen for his pain, which has provided some relief. He does not recall any other previous injury to his right ankle.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports that he is very active and enjoys playing soccer. He currently plays for the community soccer league.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right ankle pain.\n\nVITALS\n\nVitals signs look good today.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nCV: Mild edema\nNEURO: Normal sensation.\nMSK: Examination of the right ankle: Ecchymosis is present. There is swelling over the lateral malleolus. Tenderness to palpation anterolaterally in the soft tissue. No laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. No bony tenderness on palpation of the foot. Neurovascular intact. Capillary refill is less than 3 seconds with a strong dorsalis pedis pulse. Sensation is intact to light touch.\n\nRESULTS\n\n3 views of the right ankle were taken today. These reveal no evidence of fracture.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight ankle sprain.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. We discussed that his x-rays did not reveal any signs of a fracture and that he sustained a right ankle sprain. Treatment options were discussed. He was advised to keep his right lower extremity elevated, especially when seated. He will also continue to ice his right ankle. An Aircast was provided today to help stabilize his ankle. He is to also utilize crutches for the next 1 to 2 days followed by weight-bearing as tolerated. For pain control he can continue to take NSAIDs as needed. We discussed that he should see significant improvement in his symptoms over the next 2 weeks.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks to assess his progress and to determine his return to play date. He is advised to call the office should his symptoms change or worsen prior to his next appointment.\n\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
