ental smoking in the past, now lives in a smoke-free environment.\n- **Alcohol Consumption:** None.\n- **Drug Use:** None.\n- **Recreational Activities:** Enjoys cycling and outdoor activities with friends.\n- **Psychosocial:** Reports a high level of health literacy and is cooperative during the consultation. The patient has a history of making health goals with their primary care provider 2 years ago and identifies as non-binary.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- **Vital Signs:** Blood pressure within normal limits.\n- **Cardiovascular:** No murmurs, rubs, or gallops appreciated.\n- **Respiratory:** Lungs clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n- **Ophthalmologic:** No icterus noted.\n- **Neck:** No thyroid enlargement.\n- **Abdominal:** No hepatomegaly or splenomegaly appreciated.\n\n**Investigation/Test Results:**\n- **Cholesterol Level:** Elevated at 210 mg/dL (previous screening).\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\nJulie Cruz is a 17-year-old non-binary, Hispanic, imminigipated minor presenting with newly diagnosed hypercholesterolemia.\n\n**Differential Diagnosis:**\n1. Familial hypercholesterolemia.\n2. Secondary hypercholesterolemia due to nutritional habits.\n3. Possible metabolic syndrome.\n4. Hyperlipidemia secondary to thyroid dysfunction.\n5. Genetic syndromes associated with hypercholesterolemia.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n**Medical Treatment:**\n1. **Lifestyle Modification:**\n   - **Diet:** Implement a well-balanced diet focusing on foods lower in saturated fat and sugars. Patient to meet with a nutritionist for dietary counseling.\n   - **Exercise:** Encourage continuation of regular physical activity, such as cycling.\n\n2. **Follow-up:**\n   - **Cholesterol Level:** Repeat lipid panel in 3-6 months after dietary and lifestyle changes.\n   - **Specialist Referral:** Referral to a nutritionist for dietary counseling and management of hypercholesterolemia.\n   - **Genetic Testing:** Referral to Dr. Emily Thompson, a geneticist, for evaluation of potent