int (CC):**\nModerate bilateral knee pain.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nSophia Lee is a 29-year-old Asian female who presents to the specialty clinic for evaluation of bilateral knee pain. The patient reports that the pain has been persistent for the past 6 months, with no specific recall of an inciting event. The pain is described as a deep, aching pain located behind the patella, with occasional crepitus. The pain is exacerbated by activities that involve a transition from a seated to standing position and by prolonged sitting or walking. The patient notes that the pain is alleviated somewhat by rest.\n\nSophia has tried various over-the-counter measures such as ice and ace bandages but has been reluctant to take NSAIDs due to her previous use. She maintains an active lifestyle, regularly running 5-6 miles per day, which has significantly decreased to half a mile due to the knee pain. She is anxious to return to her regular running routine.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- Musculoskeletal: Positive for bilateral knee pain and occasional crepitus. Negative for swelling or redness.\n- General: Active lifestyle, decreased running distance due to knee pain.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Temperature: 98.6°F\n- Blood Pressure: 120/80 mmHg\n- Heart Rate: 72 bpm\n- Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths per minute\n\n**Physical Exam:**\n- General: Well-appearing, in no acute distress.\n- Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs.\n- Musculoskeletal: \n  - Gait: Normal gait observed.\n  - Strength: 3/5 for abduction of legs bilaterally, 5/5 for the remainder of lower extremity muscle strength.\n  - Knee: \n    - Inspection: No overlying erythema, ecchymosis, or warmth of the skin appreciated.\n    - Palpation: Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinacular retinaculum.\n    - Special Tests: Positive patellar grind test bilaterally. Pain elicited with squatting. Negative Lachman anterior and posterior drawer, McMurray tests bilaterally.\n- Neurological: Patella and achilles reflexes are symmetrical.\n\n**Investigation/Test Results:**\n- X-ray of both knees: No fractures or osteoarthritis noted.\n- MRI of both knees: Awaiting results.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n\n- **Primary Diagnosis:** M25.662 - Pain in both knees (Bilateral)\n- **Secondary Diagnosis:** Patellofemoral Pain Syndrome\n\nThe patient\'s symptoms and physical exam findings are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome. This condition is common in active young individuals and is often related to biomechanics and overuse.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n\n- **Medication:** Continue NSAIDs as needed for pain management. Sophia may take ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as required.\n- **Physical Therapy:** Referral to physical therapy for lower extremity exercises. The goal is to increase lower extremity strength, mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics.\n- **Lifestyle Change:** Advise on proper running techniques and recommend avoiding running for now to allow for healing. Continue to monitor and reassess running progression during follow-up visits.\n- **Follow-Up:** Schedule a follow-up appointment in 4 weeks to evaluate progress and adjust the treatment plan as necessary.\n\nThe patient was cooperative and eager to adhere to the treatment plan. All questions were answered, and the patient expressed understanding and agreement with the proposed management strategy. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ Sophia is a 27-year-old female who presented to the office due to bilateral knee pain that had been present for approximately six months. She was sent by her primary care physician for this complaint. Sophia was unable to recall any specific event that caused the pain. She described the pain as a deep aching pain that was located behind the kneecaps. She also stated that she would sometimes hear what seemed like creaking in her knees. The pain was exacerbated with activities such as going from a seated position to standing. Sophia works from home so she is at her desk a lot. She also experiences pain when watching TV or going up and down stairs. The pain would subside after resting for some time. At the onset of the pain, Sophia had tried putting ice on it and had gotten ace bandage like wrapping from the pharmacy. She would also take ibuprofen or aleve but tries to avoid taking any medications. Sophia is very active and tries to be as active as she can. She loves to run and would run five to six miles a day. However, she stated that she is not able to get to half a mile now and her leg will start hurting her knee will start hurting. Sophia stated that she runs both outdoors and on a treadmill. She stated that there are many knee places in Boston but she loves being outdoors and running. Sophia mentioned that she has run the Boston marathon before and it’s very nice.\nOn physical exam, Sophia had a normal BMI of 22.4 and was in no apparent distress. Her vital signs were normal. She had normal heart exam with regular rate and rhythm with no murmurs. On musculoskeletal exam, Sophia had normal gait, strength was three out of five for abduction of legs bilaterally, and the remainder of muscle strength for lower extremities were five out of five. On knee exam, there was no overlying erythema (redness), ecchymosis (bruising), or warmth of the skin appreciated. There was no effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinacular retinaculum was present. There was positive patellar grind test. Sophia was able to squat but that caused pain. Lachman anterior and posterior drawer and mcmurray tests were all negative bilaterally. Patella and achilles reflexes were symmetrical. The neuro exam was normal.\nThe x-ray of both knees showed no fractures or osteoarthritis. The mri }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nBilateral knee pain.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nSophia Jackson is a pleasant 30-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of bilateral knee pain. She was referred by her primary care provider. The onset of her pain began 6 months ago. She denies any specific injury. The patient describes her pain as a deep, achy pain. She locates her pain to the posterior aspect of her patellae. The patient also reports “creaking” in her knees. She denies any swelling or redness in her knees. Her pain is aggravated by transitioning from a seated position to standing as well as ascending and descending stairs. Her pain is alleviated by rest. The patient has been icing her knees and wrapping her knees with an ACE bandage. She also takes ibuprofen or Aleve occasionally for pain. She states she tries to be as active as she can. She enjoys running outdoors. The patient adds that she would run 5 to 6 miles per day; however, she is unable to go 0.5 miles secondary to the pain. She denies any previous injuries to her knees.\n\nShe works from home.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of an upper extremity fracture when she was in 6th grade.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain and crepitus. Denies swelling or redness in the bilateral knees.\n\nVITALS\n\nAll vital signs are within the normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nGAIT: Normal, no deformity present.\nCV: Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs.\nNo edema\nNEURO: Patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical.\nMSK: Examination of the bilateral lower extremities: Abduction strength is 3/5. Remainder of muscle strength is 5/5. Examination of the bilateral knees: No overlying erythema, ecchymosis, or warmth of the skin appreciated. No effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. Knee pain with squatting. Negative Lachman test bilaterally. Negative anterior and posterior drawer tests bilaterally. Negative McMurray test bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the bilateral knees were reviewed today. These demonstrate no evidence of any fractures or osteoarthritis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nBilateral knee patellofemoral pain syndrome.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient regarding her current symptoms. I have explained to her that her symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome. We discussed treatment options and I have recommended that we begin with conservative treatment in the form of formal physical therapy to increase her lower extremity strength, mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics. I encouraged her to take running easy for now. She can continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatories as needed for pain. All questions were answered.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me soon.\n\n###Score Rubric:\n\n1. Completeness:\n    - Does the summary include all significant components of the SOAP format?\n    - Are there any crucial aspects of the conversation missing from the summary?\n2. Accuracy:\n    - How accurately does the summary reflect the details of the conversation as they were discussed?\n    - Are there any discrepancies between what was said and what is noted?\n3. Clarity and Structure:\n    - Is the summary well-organized, following the logical flow of Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan?\n    - Is the information presented in a clear and understandable manner?\n4. Use of Medical Terminology:\n    - Is medical terminology used correctly and effectively throughout the summary?\n    - Does the use of terminology enhance the clarity and precision of the summary?\n5. Evidence-Based Support:\n    - In the Assessment and Plan sections, are the conclusions and recommendations supported by appropriate medical guidelines or literature?\n    - Does the summary demonstrate a thoughtful and knowledgeable approach to patient care?\n\n\n###Feedback: '}], 'extra_body': {}}'
