der fluoroscopy which you can think of like an x-ray to localize the exact area of your pain this should help with the inflammation that\'s causing your pain i know that sounds a bit scary it this is a typical procedure that i do all the time it\'s in office and we can get you scheduled today if you would like so what questions do you have right now\n[patient] is that something that we can do today\n[doctor] yeah i think i have time in my schedule to get it done today just you know i\'m here for you right now\n[patient] alright\n[doctor] okay\n[patient] are there are there any complications with this\n[doctor] there are sorry yeah you\'re right there are risks associated with this kind of injection just like any procedure but we aim to minimize them and my ma when she comes in we will give you some paperwork with explanations and any and hopefully answer most of the questions that you have if after reading that you have any more questions please feel free to stop us we want to make sure that you feel as comfortable as possible during this time\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] alright\n[patient] great\n[doctor] anything else\n[patient] i think that\'s it\n[doctor] alright\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ **1. Subjective:**\n\n**Chief Complaint (CC):**\nModerate to severe low back pain with radiation down the left leg.\n\n**History of Present Illness (HPI):**\nKyle Thompson, a 35-year-old African American male, presents for a routine check-up with a chief complaint of moderate to severe low back pain that radiates down the left leg. The pain started two weeks ago after lifting a box of books. He reports a sensation of a "pop" during the incident. The pain is described as aching and sharp and worsens with activity. The patient has a sedentary job and is an Olympic weightlifter with books. He has tried over-the-counter pain relief with ibuprofen and tylenol with some relief. The pain has been intermittent but has significantly limited his ability to exercise and perform daily tasks. He has not discussed surgical options previously.\n\n**Review of Systems (ROS):**\n- Musculoskeletal: Reports moderate to severe low back pain, radiating down the left leg.\n- Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling, no loss of sensation or weakness, denies bowel or bladder incontinence.\n\n**Social History:**\n- Occupation: Sedentary office job.\n- Exercise: Inconsistent, enjoys Olympic weightlifting.\n- Tobacco Use: None.\n- Alcohol Consumption: Social drinker.\n- Recreational Activities: Soccer, Manchester United fan.\n- Family History: Father with a history of back pain.\n- Education Level: Bachelor\'s degree.\n- Socio-economic Status: Upper-middle class.\n- Neighborhood: Urban, affluent, low pollution.\n\n**2. Objective:**\n\n**Vital Signs:**\n- Blood Pressure: 130/70 mmHg\n- Heart Rate: 72 bpm\n- Respiratory Rate: 18 breaths per minute\n- Temperature: Afebrile\n\n**Physical Exam:**\n- General: Alert, cooperative, in no acute distress.\n- Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, rubs, or gallops.\n- Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n- Musculoskeletal: No overlying redness or bruising on the skin. Midline tenderness at the L4-L5 disk space with right-sided lumbar paravertebral tenderness. Pain elicited with lumbar flexion and extension.\n- Neurological: Positive straight leg test (SLS) in the supine position. No radicular signs in the left leg. Reflexes: Patella and achilles reflexes are symmetrical.\n\n**Investigations:**\n- MRI Lumbar Spine: Shows disk desiccation, bulging of the disk with paracentral disk herniation resulting in moderate right neuroforaminal stenosis.\n\n**3. Assessment:**\n- Lumbar back pain at L4-L5 disk space due to herniated disk with radiculopathy.\n\n**4. Plan:**\n- **Medical Treatment:**\n  - Spinal injection with a combination of a strong NSAID (e.g., Ketorolac) and corticosteroid (e.g., Methylprednisolone) administered intrathecal route into the lumbar spine under fluoroscopy.